---
Part III: Loom in your IDE
Chapter 1: Supported editors
Language: Prose
---

# Supported editors

Loom works in your editor through its language server, the same server in every editor. It reads a loom the way Loom does, so your editor understands the prose and the code in it, and how the sections fit together. Each editor reaches the server its own way, and the guides that follow cover each one.

## Code that knows its language

Every section's code is highlighted and checked in its own language. Open `corpus/hello.loom` and misspell `greet` in the program: the error shows up under it as you type, just as it would in `hello.ts`.

The check runs on the whole file a section ends up in, not on the section alone. So `greet` is known in the program because `::[Greeting function]` brings it in, and TypeScript reads your project's `tsconfig.json` as it would for any other file. Hover and completion work the same way.

## Loom's own mistakes

Loom checks the parts that are its own, too. An anchor that names no section, two sections that share a name, and a section that pulls code from another language are all marked where you wrote them, before you run `loom tangle`.

## Moving between sections

An anchor is a link. Go to its definition and the editor jumps to the section it names. Find its references from a heading and you see every anchor that names that section.

Rename a section from its heading, and every anchor that names it follows. The outline lists your sections, so a long loom is one click from any part of it.

## A language server for each language

Loom checks a section's code with that language's own server, which Loom installs into the project when you turn the language on. Run `$ loom add typescript`, and TypeScript sections are checked from then on. Run it again after you upgrade Loom: it brings the server up to the new release, and `loom status` marks a server that an older release installed. The servers live in `.loom/services`, which `loom init` already keeps out of Git. Loom's own checks, and moving between sections, need no language turned on: they work in any loom, even one that sits in no project.

## Choose your editor

- [VS Code](/docs/loom-in-your-ide/vs-code), and the editors built on it: Cursor, Windsurf and VSCodium. They install the Loom extension.
- Zed, and Gram, its fork. They install the Loom extension.
- IntelliJ IDEA and the other JetBrains IDEs. They install the Loom plugin.
- [Helix](/docs/loom-in-your-ide/helix). `loom helix` sets it up.
- Neovim. Its own language client runs the server, with a few lines of settings.
- Any other editor with a language client. Point it at `$ loom lsp`.
